## Why Asked
This question evaluates understanding of fundamental sorting algorithms and their practical trade-offs in real-world applications.

## Key Concepts
- Divide and conquer strategies
- Partitioning vs merging approaches
- In-place vs auxiliary space requirements
- Algorithm stability and performance characteristics

## Code Example
```
// Quicksort partition
function partition(arr, low, high) {
  const pivot = arr[high];
  let i = low - 1;
  for (let j = low; j < high; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      i++;
      [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
    }
  }
  [arr[i + 1], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[i + 1]];
  return i + 1;
}

// Mergesort merge
function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;

  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] <= right[j]) {
      result.push(left[i++]);
    } else {
      result.push(right[j++]);
    }
  }

  return result.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
The Trie with prefix count mechanism addresses dynamic dictionary requirements in real-time applications like chat search, testing understanding of per-node frequency tracking, deletion correctness, and memory efficiency trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with passCount and endCount for frequency tracking
- O(length) time complexity for all operations
- Memory management through node pruning
- Correct handling of duplicates and edge cases
- Amortized deletion: pruning happens during erase, no separate cleanup needed

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.pass = 0;
    this.end = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    node.pass++;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) {
        node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.pass++;
    }
    node.end++;
  }

  exists(word) {
    const node = this._findNode(word);
    return node !== null && node.end > 0;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    const node = this._findNode(prefix);
    return node ? node.pass : 0;
  }

  erase(word) {
    if (!this.exists(word)) return false;

    let node = this.root;
    node.pass--;

    for (const ch of word) {
      const child = node.children.get(ch);
      child.pass--;
      if (child.pass === 0) {
        node.children.delete(ch);
        node = null;
        break;
      }
      node = child;
    }

    if (node) node.end--;
    return true;
  }

  _findNode(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return null;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node;
  }
}

// Usage
const trie = new Trie();
trie.insert('apple');
trie.insert('apple');
trie.insert('app');
trie.exists('apple');        // true
trie.countPrefix('app');      // 3
trie.erase('apple');
trie.exists('apple');        // true (still one left)
trie.countPrefix('app');     // 2
trie.erase('apple');
trie.exists('apple');        // false
trie.countPrefix('app');      // 1
trie.erase('nonexistent');   // false (safe)
```

## Memory Management
Nodes are allocated only when needed and automatically pruned when `pass` reaches zero during erase, ensuring no memory leaks.

## Duplicate Handling
Since `end` tracks word frequency, duplicate inserts increment the counter; duplicate erases decrement it until the count reaches zero.

## Edge Cases
- Erase on non-existent word: returns false, no changes
- Erase on word with duplicate: only decrements count, preserves other instances
- Empty string handling: root node's counters track this case

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of in-place merging, a practical skill when space is constrained.

## Key Concepts
- In-place merging with end pointers
- Time O(m+n), space O(1)
- Edge cases: empty arrays, duplicates, negative numbers

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergeInPlace(A, m, B, n) {
  let i = m - 1, j = n - 1, k = m + n - 1;
  while (j >= 0) {
    if (i >= 0 && A[i] > B[j]) {
      A[k--] = A[i--];
    } else {
      A[k--] = B[j--];
    }
  }
}
```

